<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nectanebo II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 360–342 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nectanebo II was the last native king of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, of the Thirtieth Dynasty. He took the throne around 360 BCE, repelled a Persian invasion in 351 BCE and built many temples. In 343 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a> defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelusium">Pelusium</a> and took Memphis, and he fled south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, leaving Egypt again a Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nectanebo-ii.jpg" alt="Nectanebo II" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.0 fr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Head_of_Nectanebo_II-MBA_Lyon_H1701-IMG_0204.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
